#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139702 :tutorial/content "To evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nSo, to find the value of $9^2$, we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n$2$\n\nThen, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n9^{2} = 9 \\cdot 9 = 81\n$$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $2$ is known as **squaring** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $9^2 = 81$\n- The **square** of $9$ is $81$.\n- $9$ raised to the *second* power equals $81$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c1b4655c-0ae1-50ce-a37f-b3d77fdc30d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8587 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139743 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n$3$\n\nNow, we compute the product.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2 \\times 2 = 4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 2_(⏟)_(4) \\cdot 2 &= 4 \\cdot 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8\n$$\n\nwe have $2^3=8$.\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8$.\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2cf5f36c-da57-5e4b-91e8-76c86e67837f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9457 :tutorial/title "The Cube of a Number"}]}
